# ============================================================================
# FILE: .dockerignore
#
# THINK OF THIS FILE AS: the packing list of things that must stay out of a
# Docker build shipment.
#
# When Docker builds Dockerfile.api or Dockerfile.web, it first sends the project
# directory to the Docker engine as the build context. Each pattern below means
# do not send this. That makes builds faster and keeps local clutter out.
#
# THIS IS DIFFERENT FROM .gitignore:
#   .gitignore      -> controls what Git records in the repository
#   .dockerignore   -> controls what Docker can see while building an image
#
# A file can need both protections. The `.env` file, for example, should be
# neither committed nor copied into an image because it can contain passwords
# and API keys. Runtime settings are supplied by docker-compose.yml instead.
#
# BUILD FLOW:
#   docker compose build (docker-compose.yml)
#     -> send the directory, minus the patterns HERE
#       -> run Dockerfile.api or Dockerfile.web
#         -> produce the API/worker or web image
#
# WHO USES IT / WHAT LIVES HERE: Docker reads this file automatically for the
# builds defined by docker-compose.yml, Dockerfile.api, and Dockerfile.web.
# ============================================================================
# Git history and internal bookkeeping are unnecessary inside a running
# container and can make the build context much larger.
.git/
# Python virtual environments contain packages installed for one developer's
# operating system. Dockerfile.api installs a clean, reproducible set from
# requirements.txt instead.
.venv/
venv/
# Generated Python bytecode and caches. Python recreates these whenever needed;
# sending them would only add stale, machine-specific files.
__pycache__/
# Test, type-checker, and lint caches produced by pytest, mypy, and ruff. None is
# application source, and none is needed by Dockerfile.api or Dockerfile.web.
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
ruff_cache/
# Local coverage reports produced by the development tools in
# requirements-dev.txt. CI may generate these again, but the app never reads them.
htmlcov/
.coverage
# Local secrets and per-machine settings. docker-compose.yml passes environment
# values into containers at runtime, after the image has already been built.
.env
# Captured server output from local debugging. Useful to the developer who made
# it, but unrelated to the application files copied by the Dockerfiles.
server.out.log
server.err.log
